 ### [Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching](2312-adaptive-step-size-ray-marching.md)
-Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching optimizes computational cost by varying the sampling interval along a ray based on local density changes and transmittance. It uses fine steps in high-density regions and coarse steps in empty space or at distance. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md) by explicitly coupling step size to density gradients and transmittance thresholds, and from [Variance-based Step Size Adaptation](13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md) by relying on deterministic density thresholds rather than statistical variance.
+Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching is a volume rendering technique that optimizes computational cost by varying the sampling interval along a ray based on local density changes and transmittance. Unlike fixed-step linear sampling, which forces a trade-off between quality and efficiency, this method uses fine steps in high-density regions and coarse steps in empty space or at distance. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md) by explicitly coupling step size to density gradients and transmittance thresholds, and from [Variance-based Step Size Adaptation](13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md) by relying on deterministic density thresholds rather than statistical variance.
 
 ### [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md)
-Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment accelerates traversal through empty or homogeneous regions of a volume by exponentially increasing the step size when no density is detected. It solves the problem of redundant sampling in sparse areas, such as the sky in atmospheric rendering, by skipping large distances until a feature is encountered. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching](2312-adaptive-step-size-ray-marching.md) by using a binary "empty/non-empty" heuristic rather than continuous gradient or variance metrics to drive step changes, and from [Variance-based Step Size Adaptation](13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md) by relying on mip-level offsets rather than statistical error bounds.
+Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment is a ray marching optimization technique that accelerates traversal through empty or homogeneous regions of a volume by exponentially increasing the step size when no density is detected. It solves the problem of redundant sampling in sparse areas, such as the sky in atmospheric rendering, by skipping large distances until a feature is encountered. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching](2312-adaptive-step-size-ray-marching.md) by using a binary "empty/non-empty" heuristic rather than continuous gradient or variance metrics to drive step changes, and from [Variance-based Step Size Adaptation](13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md) by relying on mip-level offsets rather than statistical error bounds.
 
